{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Downtrend bias: when the recent 10–30d history is falling, the model stays too high versus realized prices. Apply a negative level shift to the whole path (typically ~-1 to -3 €/t early, widening to ~-3 to -8 €/t mid/late horizon).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: forecast error grows with lead time (errors are smaller in days 1–5, larger in days 10–30). Use an increasing correction with horizon (e.g., subtract an extra ~0.1–0.2 €/t per forecast day during strong downtrends).",
    "Insufficient downside momentum: the model underestimates the speed/depth of selloffs and prints a path that is too smooth. If last 5–10d slope is strongly negative, steepen the forecast slope downward (accelerate declines in days 1–15).",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: after sharp drops, the model often forecasts a stronger rebound than realized (late-horizon forecasts jump back toward/above prior levels while true stays lower). Cap rebound magnitude and enforce slower mean reversion upward.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model reverts to a too-high long-run mean in bearish regimes. Replace the anchor with a regime-conditioned mean (e.g., use recent 20–30d average minus a bearish discount) rather than the unconditional mean.",
    "Peak/trough timing lag: turning points are predicted too early on the upside (rebound arrives earlier than realized). Delay any upward inflection by several days (≈3–7d) following a drawdown."
  ]
}
